"""Explicit industrial/fisheries content; named Namibian places, synthetic teaching data."""
from assemble_topic import assemble
FACTS={
'01':['Primary activities obtain natural raw materials, secondary activities process/manufacture and tertiary activities provide services.','An integrated fishing chain can cross all three categories, so classification follows the particular activity.'],
'01-01':['Catching fish from a natural stock is primary activity.','Modern equipment or large scale does not by itself turn extraction into manufacturing.'],
'01-02':['Filleting, freezing or canning fish is secondary processing.','Secondary refers to transformation, not second-hand products.'],
'01-03':['Transport, repair, marketing and finance are tertiary services even when their customers are primary producers.','A service can need expensive equipment without becoming manufacturing.'],
'02':['Raw material, labour, energy, capital, transport, market and site factors interact in industrial location.','A factor needs an operational or cost link to explain attraction rather than a name alone.'],
'02-01':['Perishability and reliable catch access can favour proximity to landings under suitable handling conditions.','Nearby supply that is inadequate or unsustainable is not made sufficient merely by short distance.'],
'02-02':['Industrial labour needs include suitable skills, availability and training, not population size alone.','A relevant skill cluster can reduce recruitment/repair difficulties while also creating competition for workers.'],
'02-03':['Reliable energy supports refrigeration and machinery, with interruption risks distinct from tariff.','Backup can reduce some risks but requires feasible investment and maintenance.'],
'02-04':['Capital finances plant/equipment and working needs, while access depends on viable arrangements.','A nearby bank does not establish that every proposal will receive finance.'],
'02-05':['Transport suitability, cold-chain continuity, time and reliability affect a fish plant’s connections.','Short distance alone does not establish an effective route.'],
'02-06':['Markets involve buyers with product, quality, timing and price requirements.','A large nearby population does not automatically demand the plant’s specific output on viable terms.'],
'02-07':['Site includes the plot’s space, ground, drainage, utilities and expansion conditions.','Situation concerns relationships to places and networks, such as a port or market.'],
'03':['Coastal upwelling can supply nutrients to the lit surface layer, supporting phytoplankton and marine food webs.','Cold current or coast length alone does not establish a sustainable catch.'],
'03-01':['The Benguela marine setting influences Namibia’s coastal temperature, productivity and fish distribution.','Species responses to changing water properties differ, so catch relocation is not identical to whole-stock decline.'],
'03-02':['Upwelling replaces displaced surface water with deeper water that can be nutrient-rich.','The process supports producer growth under suitable conditions rather than lifting adult fish directly into nets.'],
'03-03':['Phytoplankton support consumers and fish through food-web links, with growth and survival over time.','Nutrients and sunlight have different roles and not all primary production becomes a human catch.'],
'03-04':['Coast length provides possible extent and access but habitat, shelf conditions, productivity and services also matter.','Fish are unevenly distributed, so a longer coastline is not proof of greater resource availability.'],
'04':['Demersal fish occur mainly near the seabed while pelagic fish use the water column away from it.','Capture methods need to match target habitat, behaviour and the relevant management conditions.'],
'04-01':['Hake provides a Namibian demersal context, with near-bottom methods including suitable trawls or lines.','Demersal habitat is relative to the bed, not simply distance from shore.'],
'04-02':['Horse mackerel provides a Namibian pelagic context, while water-column schools can be targeted with suitable gear.','Pelagic does not mean every species remains at the surface throughout its life.'],
'04-03':['Trawling tows a net, purse seining encircles a school and closes underneath, and longlining uses baited hooks.','Gear efficiency for catching and sustainability of amount/timing are distinct judgements.'],
'05':['Harmful blooms, water changes, pollution and excessive extraction affect fish through different pathways.','Management needs evidence, suitable limits/selectivity and actual compliance rather than a conservation label alone.'],
'05-01':['Harmful algal events can affect organisms through toxins or other pathways such as oxygen depletion after decay.','Not every bloom is harmful or red, and phytoplankton also form a food-web foundation.'],
'05-02':['Water-temperature changes can alter habitat suitability, reproduction and distribution.','Catch changes need effort and survey context before being interpreted as a unique stock or environmental effect.'],
'05-03':['Pollution impacts depend on substance, source, pathway, concentration and receiving environment.','A port or plant’s presence alone does not prove a particular pollution event.'],
'05-04':['Overfishing concerns extraction relative to sustainable stock production/recovery, not simply a large catch.','In the simplified 100 initial +40 replenishment −60 removal exercise, stock ends at 80 units.'],
'05-05':['Namibia’s Marine Resources Act, 2000 provides a named framework including total-allowable-catch and quota mechanisms.','A legal framework needs suitable implementation and does not itself prove compliance or resource recovery.'],
'05-06':['A quota specifies permitted extraction under a defined system and period, distinct from a catch forecast.','An appropriate limit and actual compliance are separate conditions for the intended resource outcome.'],
'05-07':['Suitable net size/type can affect juvenile retention, bycatch and habitat interaction under relevant conditions.','Selectivity is conditional on gear and fish, not a promise of catching only desired organisms.'],
'06':['Fish-processing location depends on catch access, power, labour, finance, transport, markets and suitable site.','The relative importance differs between fresh-local and frozen-distant product chains.'],
'07':['An industrial system connects inputs, transforming processes and outputs within a stated boundary.','Outputs include products and residual/by-product streams requiring appropriate destinations.'],
'07-01':['Fish, labour, water, energy, equipment and packaging can be inputs to processing.','Different products require different input combinations and quantities.'],
'07-02':['Receiving, checking, cleaning, filleting, freezing, packing and storage are possible processing stages according to the product.','A bottleneck affects linked stages and should be matched to the actual capacity or service shortfall.'],
'07-03':['Main products, usable by-products and material needing management are distinct output streams.','In the simplified balance, 1,000 kg input corresponds to 600 kg main product, 250 kg trimmings and 150 kg other material.'],
'08':['Agglomeration clusters related activities through shared services, skills and connections.','A list of jobs or exports alone does not explain the spatial links drawing activities together.'],
'09':['Markets, supply/maintenance, transport and finance support different functions in the fishing chain.','The immediate bottleneck should determine the most relevant service response.'],
'09-01':['Local and foreign buyers can require different products, timing and handling.','Market access needs demand and delivery conditions rather than an assumed guaranteed sale.'],
'09-02':['Parts, fuel and technical support can reduce operational interruptions when suitable and available.','Nearby service without the required part is not necessarily more useful than a dependable farther supplier.'],
'09-03':['Cold-chain transport includes handling and transfer stages as well as the principal journey.','A suitable vehicle does not correct an unsuitable delay outside it.'],
'09-04':['Working finance can bridge input payments and later receipts under feasible conditions.','In the supplied timing exercise, N$80,000 early payments against N$10,000 available create a N$70,000 gap.'],
'10':['Fishing can support food, employment, services and trade when resources and operations remain viable.','Sustainable-development claims need resource, social, economic and environmental evidence over time.']}
CARDS={
'001':('Name the industry category that processes raw material into products.','Secondary industry.',['1 mark for the name secondary.']),
'002':('Describe the activity chain: A catches fish, B makes frozen fillets, C transports the packed product.','A is primary extraction, B secondary processing and C a tertiary service. The category follows each operation rather than the shared fish product.',['1 mark for A’s extraction feature; 1 for B’s transformation evidence; 1 for C’s service relationship.']),
'003':('Describe the primary-activity evidence: a crew obtains fish from a natural marine stock and lands them without the described factory transformation.','The operation obtains natural raw material. That supports primary classification. Its use of modern vessels would not by itself make it secondary manufacturing.',['1 mark for obtaining-resource feature; 1 for the catch/landing evidence; 1 for the activity-not-technology interpretation.']),
'004':('Describe the secondary-activity evidence: a plant receives whole fish and converts them into packaged frozen fillets.','The plant transforms the input. Filleting/freezing/packing are processing in the stated operation. Secondary refers to that transformation, not used goods.',['1 mark for the transformation feature; 1 for the process evidence; 1 for the correct category meaning.']),
'005':('Describe why a vessel-repair service remains tertiary even though most customers are fishing operators.','It provides maintenance rather than extracting fish or manufacturing the stated product. Its customers’ primary activity does not change the service classification. The link supports the fishing chain.',['1 mark for the service feature; 1 for the customer/activity evidence distinction; 1 for the support relationship.']),
'006':('Explain how raw-material perishability and reliable power can attract fish processing to a suitable location.','Shorter dependable access to landings can reduce handling delays for perishable catch. Reliable power supports freezing and machinery, reducing interruption risk. Their combined fit can favour the site, subject to other needs.',['1 mark for perishability as a factor; 1 for the access/delay link; 1 for power reliability; 1 for its process/quality effect.']),
'007':('Describe the cost comparison for the same output: A has monthly stated costs N$48,000 delivery+N$12,000 dispatch+N$20,000 energy; B N$74,000+N$9,000+N$18,000.','A’s stated total is N$80,000 versus B’s N$101,000, a N$21,000 advantage. A’s raw-material-delivery saving outweighs its higher other stated items. This is a partial-cost comparison, not guaranteed profit or full feasibility.',['1 mark for the total-cost pattern with values; 1 for the cost-component evidence; 1 for the limited-scope interpretation.']),
'008':('Name the input-location factor made especially important by perishable catch and delivery time.','Access to raw materials or fish landings.',['1 mark for the factor name: raw-material access.']),
'009':('Name the labour characteristic needed for refrigeration repairs, beyond simply a large population.','Relevant technical skills or training.',['1 mark for a relevant named skill/training feature.']),
'010':('Name the energy characteristic that limits interruption risks, distinct from its tariff.','Reliability of power supply.',['1 mark for the name reliability.']),
'011':('Name the resource needed to finance plant equipment and working needs.','Capital or suitable finance.',['1 mark for the name capital/finance.']),
'012':('Name the transport requirement that preserves temperature-controlled products through handling and delivery.','A suitable continuous cold chain.',['1 mark for the requirement name cold chain.']),
'013':('Name the location factor concerning buyers and demand for the specific product.','Markets or market access.',['1 mark for the name markets/market access.']),
'014':('Name the location term for the plot’s own space, slope, ground and drainage characteristics.','Site.',['1 mark for the name site.']),
'015':('Explain the link from coastal upwelling to a useful fish resource.','Upwelling can bring nutrient-rich deeper water into the lit surface layer. Nutrients support phytoplankton where other needs are met. Consumers and fish use that production through food-web links, supporting resource availability over time.',['1 mark for upwelling as the factor; 1 for the nutrient/sunlit-layer link; 1 for producer-to-consumer transfer; 1 for the conditional fish-resource effect.']),
'016':('Describe the resource interpretation: A has recorded upwelling, nutrients and suitable habitat; B has more coast kilometres but no equivalent productivity information.','A has a supported biological-production pathway. B’s greater length alone does not establish more fish. Sustainable catches at either still need stock and management evidence.',['1 mark for A’s nutrient/production feature; 1 for the length/productivity evidence contrast; 1 for the harvest-limit interpretation.']),
'017':('Describe the ocean-current interpretation: a cold coastal system changes temperature and species distribution, while catches shift between areas.','The marine setting can modify habitat and where fish are found. Shifted catches are compatible with distribution change. They do not alone prove the same whole-stock decline everywhere.',['1 mark for the current/habitat relationship; 1 for spatial-catch evidence; 1 for the distribution-versus-stock limitation.']),
'018':('Describe the upwelling sequence shown by this record: surface water moves away, deeper cold nutrient-rich water rises, and producer growth increases in the lit layer.','Deeper water replaces displaced surface water. Nutrients reach the illuminated layer. The recorded producer response supports upwelling’s food-web contribution, not adult fish being lifted into nets.',['1 mark for the replacement-flow pattern; 1 for the nutrient/light evidence; 1 for the biological interpretation.']),
'019':('Describe the feeding relationship: phytoplankton use light and nutrients, zooplankton consume them and fish feed at later levels.','Producers form a food-web foundation. Consumer links transfer material and energy towards fish. Growth and survival take time; nutrients do not instantly become adult catch.',['1 mark for the producer feature; 1 for the feeding-link evidence; 1 for the time/production interpretation.']),
'020':('Describe what a coastline-length record can establish if it gives kilometres but no habitat, productivity or stock data.','It establishes coastal extent, not a uniform fish density. Resource availability also needs habitat/productivity evidence. A sustainable-catch ranking cannot be calculated from length alone.',['1 mark for the extent feature; 1 for the missing-resource evidence; 1 for the limit on catch inference.']),
'021':('Describe the capture sequence of purse seining.','Locate and surround a suitable school with a net. Close the lower opening. Retrieve the retained catch under the appropriate operating conditions.',['1 mark for the first encirclement step; 1 for closing underneath; 1 for retrieval as the result.']),
'022':('Describe the method contrasts: A tows a net near the seabed; B tows through a mid-water school; C surrounds a school and closes below it.','A supports bottom/demersal trawling, B mid-water pelagic trawling and C purse seining. Depth and net movement distinguish the operations.',['1 mark for A’s bottom-tow feature; 1 for B’s water-column evidence; 1 for C’s encircling/closure relationship.']),
'023':('Describe habitat classification: fish A is recorded within 5 m of the bed in 200 m water, while B schools at 30 m where the bed is also 200 m down.','A is near-bottom and consistent with demersal habitat. B is in the water column away from the bed, consistent with pelagic habitat. Shared total depth does not make them the same setting.',['1 mark for A’s near-bed feature; 1 for B’s depth/bed evidence; 1 for the relative-habitat interpretation.']),
'024':('Describe the pelagic interpretation: the resource identifies a school away from the seabed, with a net towed through its mid-water level.','The target occupies the water column rather than the bed. The net depth matches that record. It supports a pelagic mid-water operation, not automatic bottom contact.',['1 mark for the water-column feature; 1 for net/fish depth evidence; 1 for the matched-method interpretation.']),
'025':('Describe the gear difference: A tows an open net through a reach; B deploys an encircling net and closes its bottom; C uses multiple baited hooks.','A is trawling, B purse seining and C longlining. Their catch mechanisms differ: towing, encircling/closing and hooking.',['1 mark for A’s towing feature; 1 for B’s closure evidence; 1 for C’s hook relationship.']),
'026':('Name one physical environmental threat to fish availability, distinct from excessive extraction.','A harmful algal event; suitable temperature or oxygen changes are also valid.',['1 mark for a relevant physical/environmental threat name.']),
'027':('How far do you agree that a quota alone proves sustainable use? The limit fits a supplied assessment, but actual recorded catch exceeds it and pollution observations remain unresolved. Judge extraction and wider resource condition.','Disagree with proof. A suitable limit is relevant, but excess actual catch shows a compliance problem. Pollution is another unresolved pathway. Sustainable use needs effective removal control and relevant environmental assessment, not the quota’s presence alone.',['Up to 4 C marks for judging the assessment, actual-catch evidence, compliance mechanism and unresolved environmental pressure.','1 C mark for a supported decision against the stated resource criterion.']),
'028':('Name the broad event often called red tide that can harm fish through toxins or other pathways.','A harmful algal bloom.',['1 mark for the name harmful algal bloom.']),
'029':('Name one water property whose fluctuation can alter fish habitat and distribution.','Temperature; an appropriately contextualised oxygen change is also relevant.',['1 mark for a relevant water-property name.']),
'030':('Name one pollutant pathway from a processing operation that needs appropriate management.','Discharge of unsuitable untreated wastewater into connected waters.',['1 mark for a relevant discharge/pathway name.']),
'031':('Name the pressure involving excessive removal relative to sustainable stock recovery.','Overfishing.',['1 mark for the name overfishing.']),
'032':('Name the Namibian marine-resource legal framework used as the studied example.','The Marine Resources Act, 2000.',['1 mark for the correct Act name/year.']),
'033':('Name the management mechanism specifying a permitted extraction amount for a defined resource/period.','A quota, within the relevant total-allowable-catch framework.',['1 mark for the name quota.']),
'034':('Name the gear characteristic that can affect which illustrated fish sizes pass through a net.','Mesh size.',['1 mark for the name mesh size.']),
'035':('Explain why a fish processor can benefit from being near suitable landings and dependable cold-chain services.','Nearby suitable landings reduce raw-material handling time. Cold-chain services help preserve quality during storage and transfer. The combination supports the required input and product conditions, provided supply and services are adequate.',['1 mark for proximity as a factor; 1 for the delivery-time link; 1 for cold-chain service; 1 for the conditional quality/continuity effect.']),
'036':('Describe the site trade-off: A is near landings with reliable power but limited expansion space; B is farther away with more land and a longer refrigerated journey.','A has input-time and power advantages but a space constraint. B has expansion room with a longer cold journey. The choice depends on scale, cold-chain costs and future needs, not distance alone.',['1 mark for A’s paired feature pattern; 1 for B’s evidence; 1 for the conditional trade-off interpretation.']),
'037':('Describe a fish-processing sequence from receiving catch to a frozen packed product.','Receive and check the fish, then clean and prepare it by the required operations. Freeze under suitable conditions. Pack and store for dispatch, directing residual material to its appropriate management route.',['1 mark for the first receiving/preparation step; 1 for freezing; 1 for the packed/stored output and residual route as the result.']),
'038':('Describe the input–process–output relationships in the supplied original processing-system diagram, using its boundary and labelled material/service flows.','Fish and supporting resources enter; preparation/freezing/packing transform or handle them; saleable product and residual/by-product streams leave. The output destinations and units must follow the actual diagram.',['1 mark for the input feature; 1 for the transforming-stage evidence; 1 for the output/destination relationship.']),
'039':('Describe the input evidence: a plant receives fish, packaging, water and energy, while labour and equipment support operation.','The system uses more than raw fish. Material, energy and service/capacity inputs support different needs. Their units differ and should not be combined into one fish-mass total.',['1 mark for the varied-input pattern; 1 for named input evidence; 1 for the category/unit interpretation.']),
'040':('Describe the process bottleneck: fish can be delivered and stored, but the freezing stage cannot handle the arrival rate.','The constraint is freezing throughput, not merely incoming storage. Material can accumulate before that stage. Adding storage alone would not raise freezing output per hour.',['1 mark for the stage-capacity feature; 1 for input/backlog evidence; 1 for the targeted-response interpretation.']),
'041':('Describe the output balance: a stated stage receives 1,000 kg fish and produces 600 kg main product,250 kg usable trimmings and150 kg other material, with no other mass change.','The outputs sum to 1,000 kg. Main-product yield is 60% by mass. Residual streams need destinations; the yield is not a profit margin.',['1 mark for the balanced-output pattern; 1 for the 600/1,000 evidence; 1 for the mass-versus-finance distinction.']),
'042':('Explain how a cluster of fish operations and specialist repairs can reinforce a port town’s industrial attraction.','Several operators create demand for specialist repairs. Nearby services and skills can reduce some maintenance delays. Easier access can attract related activity, reinforcing the cluster under suitable conditions.',['1 mark for shared demand as a factor; 1 for the service-location link; 1 for the delay/access effect; 1 for the reinforcing agglomeration mechanism.']),
'043':('Describe the cluster relationship: landings, processors, cold storage and repairs are nearby, with recorded service links among them.','The related activities are spatially concentrated. The service links support access and continuity. This is agglomeration evidence, not merely a count of jobs.',['1 mark for the clustering pattern; 1 for the linked-service evidence; 1 for the access/continuity interpretation.']),
'044':('Name one tertiary service necessary to keep processing machinery working.','Maintenance or repair services.',['1 mark for the name maintenance/repairs.']),
'045':('Describe the service bottleneck: a plant has catch and orders, but dispatch is delayed by failed refrigeration and unavailable spare parts.','Maintenance and supply are immediate constraints. They affect cold storage/dispatch despite available inputs and buyers. More marketing alone does not repair the documented failure.',['1 mark for the service-constraint feature; 1 for the failure/parts evidence; 1 for the targeted-response interpretation.']),
'046':('Name the service function connecting fish products to local or foreign buyers with particular demands.','Marketing or market access/distribution.',['1 mark for a relevant buyer-connection service name.']),
'047':('Name one supply needed to restore a machine with a documented failed component.','A suitable replacement part, with appropriate technical support.',['1 mark for the suitable part/support name.']),
'048':('Name the service linking packed products to buyers while meeting required handling conditions.','Suitable transportation or cold-chain distribution.',['1 mark for the relevant service name.']),
'049':('Name the finance need arising when input bills must be paid before sales receipts arrive.','Working capital or working finance.',['1 mark for the name working capital/finance.']),
'050':('Explain why fishing employment alone is not sufficient evidence of sustainable development.','Jobs can support incomes, but continued benefit depends on the resource and viable operations. Excess extraction or unmanaged pollution can undermine future supply and functions. Sustainable-development judgement needs those linked conditions, not one employment count alone.',['1 mark for employment as a benefit factor; 1 for the resource-continuity link; 1 for a pressure affecting recovery; 1 for its longer-term development effect.']),
'051':('Describe what this record supports: a new plant reports jobs, but there are no stock, compliance or environmental-performance observations.','The record supports employment creation. It leaves key resource and environmental conditions unmeasured. It therefore does not establish the broader sustainable-development claim.',['1 mark for the employment feature; 1 for the missing-evidence pattern; 1 for the bounded interpretation.']),
'052':('Explain the error in classifying a filleting/freezing plant as primary just because it uses fish. Compare it with catching fish and transporting packages.','The confusion classifies by raw material instead of activity. Catching is primary, processing is secondary and transport tertiary. The test identifies extraction, transformation or service in the described operation.',['1 mark for the material/activity confusion; 1 for the correct three-way distinction applied as a test.']),
'053':('Explain why a list of jobs and exports does not itself explain fishing-industry agglomeration. What evidence would discriminate a cluster mechanism?','The error substitutes outcomes for spatial links. Agglomeration requires related activities clustering through advantages such as shared skills, repairs or cold storage. A map or record of those usable links provides the test.',['1 mark for the outcome/mechanism confusion; 1 for the correct clustering/link distinction and discriminating evidence test.'])}
TASKS=[
{'objectives':['01','02','04','07'],'prompt':'(a) Name the industry sector that catches fish. [1]\n(b) Describe three distinct factors that can favour locating a fish-processing plant close to a suitable landing port. [3]\n(c) Name the service sector that carries packed fish to buyers. [1]\n(d) Describe the main-product yield and another output in this processing record: 1,000 kg input gives 600 kg main product, 250 kg usable trimmings and 150 kg other material. [2]','answer':'(a) Primary. (b) Shorter movement of perishable raw fish can limit delay; suitable cold-chain/power services support handling; access to appropriate labour or dispatch links supports operations. Credit three relevant location descriptions, not a repeated transport idea. (c) Tertiary. (d) The main-product yield is 60% of the input mass; usable trimmings account for another 250 kg. This is a mass record, not a profit calculation.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for primary.','(b) 3 A marks for three distinct relevant location point descriptions.','(c) 1 A mark for tertiary.','(d) 2 B marks for the yield interpretation and distinct output evidence.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['02','06','09'],'prompt':'A hypothetical processor compares sites. Monthly inbound delivery, outbound dispatch and energy costs are respectively A: N$48,000, N$12,000 and N$20,000; B: N$74,000, N$9,000 and N$18,000. No other cost or revenue is supplied.\n(a) Name the location factor represented by dispatch costs. [1]\n(b) Calculate the total of the three supplied monthly costs at A. [2]\n(c) Describe three contrasts between the sites using this resource. [3]\n(d) What decision about the claim “A must be more profitable” is supported? [1]','answer':'(a) Transport. (b) 48,000+12,000+20,000 = N$80,000 per month. (c) A has N$26,000 lower inbound cost, but N$3,000 higher dispatch and N$2,000 higher energy costs. Its supplied total is N$21,000 below B’s N$101,000. Credit any three distinct supported comparisons. (d) A has a lower supplied partial cost total, but profit cannot be established without revenue and remaining costs.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for transport.','(b) 2 B marks for the addition method and correct monthly total with currency.','(c) 3 B marks for three different comparative patterns using resource evidence and values; do not credit the same comparison twice.','(d) 1 C mark for a supported decision distinguishing partial costs from profit.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Calculate',2,'B'),('c','Describe',3,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['05','10'],'prompt':'How far do you agree that a quota on paper proves sustainable fishing? In this hypothetical simplified stock account, opening stock is 100 units, net natural addition before fishing is 40 units, and actual removal is 60 units; other stock changes are stated absent. The operator observes its 60-unit quota and reports jobs. Judge maintained stock and the wider sustainability claim. [5]','answer':'Disagree that those facts prove sustainability. Closing stock is 80 units, a decline of 20 despite quota compliance. This quota is therefore not sufficient to maintain stock under the stated conditions; the permitted removal and its scientific basis need reassessment. Jobs are a development benefit but do not establish recovery, ecosystem condition or future livelihood security. Monitoring, an appropriate limit and effective enforcement are relevant together. The simplified stock balance supports a decline for this interval, not a complete prediction of the real fishery.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judging the 100+40−60 balance, the compliant-but-inappropriate limit, employment evidence and the missing wider ecological/longer-term evidence.','1 C mark for a supported overall decision distinguishing a stated rule from observed sustainable performance.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]}
]
SOURCES=[('NAMIBIA-MARINE-RESOURCES-ACT','https://www.npc.gov.na/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/Marine-Resources-Act-2000.pdf'),('NAMPORT-COLD-STORAGE','https://www.namport.com.na/services/cold-storage-facilities/464/'),('NAMPORT-LUDERITZ','https://www.namport.com.na/ports/welcome-to-the-port-of-luderitz/523/'),('NOAA-HARMFUL-BLOOMS','https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/habharm.html'),('NOAA-RED-TIDE','https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/redtide.html'),('NASA-NAMIB','https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/namibia-3464/')]
if __name__=='__main__':assemble('2.1',FACTS,CARDS,TASKS,SOURCES)
